1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:08,280
A few people ask me what you're actually meant to do with Scout when your team's on defense, or getting completely rolled, or you're playing on some narrow map like Dust Bowl, where there's barely anywhere to move.

2
00:00:08,460 --> 00:00:12,000
And they all sound like different questions, but it's pretty much the same problem.

3
00:00:12,260 --> 00:00:15,160
You can't just hold W and take whatever fight you want anymore.

4
00:00:15,560 --> 00:00:18,440
Because Scout feels ridiculous when your team's doing well.

5
00:00:18,800 --> 00:00:25,180
Everyone's distracted, people are getting separated, you've got space to run around, and you're usually the first person there whenever a fight opens up.

6
00:00:25,180 --> 00:00:29,300
But the second your team loses all that pressure and every doorway starts getting watched

7
00:00:29,660 --> 00:00:35,000
Scout can go from feeling like he has no limitations to feeling like one of the most useless classes in the game

8
00:00:38,560 --> 00:00:42,560
So a lot of this video is going to be about slowing down a bit using chip damage

9
00:00:42,680 --> 00:00:47,220
Sitting further back on high ground where you can actually see what's going on and waiting for someone to overextend

10
00:00:47,440 --> 00:00:50,760
Instead of launching yourself into three people every time you see their backs turned

11
00:00:50,760 --> 00:00:55,420
Because even when an opening looks really good, you still have to think about whether your team is alive,

12
00:00:55,500 --> 00:00:59,480
whether anyone can actually help you, and whether dying for that play is even worth it.

13
00:00:59,480 --> 00:01:05,960
Especially when you're defending on red in payload or attack defend, because you can't take your life for granted like Blue Team Cat, because

14
00:01:06,000 --> 00:01:09,920
those respawn times on certain maps get just ridiculous, and

15
00:01:10,360 --> 00:01:14,140
one bad death can take you out of the fight for what feels like forever.

16
00:01:14,640 --> 00:01:20,200
And Scout can create way more for his team than the scoreboard actually shows. You can make half the enemy team turn around,

17
00:01:20,200 --> 00:01:24,440
pull someone away from the objective or force their medic backwards but your

18
00:01:24,440 --> 00:01:28,180
team still has to notice and do something with it. So we're gonna go

19
00:01:28,180 --> 00:01:31,060
through all of that with some pre-recorded clips that I'm gonna lightly

20
00:01:31,060 --> 00:01:34,800
commentate and how to stay useful when you can't hold W, how to know when to

21
00:01:34,800 --> 00:01:39,320
commit and how to tell when you made a bad play versus when your team just

22
00:01:39,320 --> 00:01:43,900
didn't take advantage of what you created. In downtime situations like this

23
00:01:43,900 --> 00:01:47,260
on defense, chip damage is sometimes all you can really do but it's more

24
00:01:47,260 --> 00:01:50,640
important than people think. It makes people walk back, slows them down and

25
00:01:50,640 --> 00:01:54,260
forces them to look for heals. And make sure not just when pestering people but

26
00:01:54,260 --> 00:01:57,220
in general make sure you're on an elevated platform when holding any

27
00:01:57,220 --> 00:02:00,940
position with Scout. It's crucial that if you get the chance to not let engineers

28
00:02:00,940 --> 00:02:04,360
get their sentry built in the first place. If it's level 3 you alert your

29
00:02:04,360 --> 00:02:07,540
team and when your combo comes out to deal with it help them if you can at

30
00:02:07,540 --> 00:02:11,180
that very moment but otherwise pay attention to the flanks. The engineer was

31
00:02:11,180 --> 00:02:14,800
on the ball here and he starts building again so I call it out to my soldier

32
00:02:14,800 --> 00:02:16,800
and he deals with it straight away.

33
00:02:17,860 --> 00:02:22,680
The engineer is probably thinking, oh my god, I'm so unlucky, they keep walking out as soon as I build my stuff.

34
00:02:22,720 --> 00:02:25,500
But it isn't luck, I'm scouting out these areas,

35
00:02:25,500 --> 00:02:45,460
I being an actual scout and I communicating what I see to my team At the same time I not overextending I trading these two flanks like they my post I contesting them and I cleaning up anyone who tries to walk through But I not running forward trying to take a 1v2 I just taking it slow taking my time and making sure these areas stay contested because after all we playing defense

36
00:02:45,460 --> 00:02:50,180
We're not the ones attacking. You don't need to hold W as much as you might think just because you're playing Scout.

37
00:02:59,260 --> 00:03:07,640
Right here I'm backed into a corner by a pyro and a scout.

38
00:03:07,640 --> 00:03:11,480
It doesn't look like I have anywhere to go but there's this tiny slope in the ground.

39
00:03:11,480 --> 00:03:15,720
I run up it, jump backwards and it gives me just enough height to double jump forward

40
00:03:15,720 --> 00:03:17,060
over the pyro's head.

41
00:03:17,060 --> 00:03:20,920
That tiny bit of elevation saves me from getting flamed and just happens to set me up to kill

42
00:03:20,920 --> 00:03:22,140
the scout as well.

43
00:03:22,140 --> 00:03:25,640
That's one of the best skills you can have as a scout, not just seamlessly using anything

44
00:03:25,640 --> 00:03:30,020
around you, but identifying what can be used as high ground in the first place.

45
00:03:30,020 --> 00:03:33,660
It's a bit like Emmett becoming a master builder at the end of the Lego movie, except you're

46
00:03:33,660 --> 00:03:39,400
looking at every box, railing or tiny slope and thinking, can I use it as high ground?

47
00:03:39,400 --> 00:03:42,1000
Basically any type of elevated platform will let you get extra height and reposition with

48
00:03:42,1000 --> 00:03:46,280
less risk.

49
00:03:46,280 --> 00:03:49,780
Like the last clip, this is a defensive situation that isn't as straightforward as holding

50
00:03:49,780 --> 00:03:52,460
a boxed in last with two obvious flanks.

51
00:03:52,460 --> 00:03:55,480
When you're forced to fight in more open areas without much pressure from your team,

52
00:03:55,480 --> 00:03:58,960
the scout needs to take some of that pressure off them by becoming the centre of attention

53
00:03:58,960 --> 00:04:00,340
without just throwing his life away.

54
00:04:00,340 --> 00:04:04,600
It's about occupying spaces the enemy can't ignore, forcing them to deal with you and

55
00:04:04,600 --> 00:04:16,860
drawing attention away from your team while still prioritising survivability.

56
00:04:16,860 --> 00:04:20,360
Once your team is no longer occupying the space you were trying to defend and the enemy

57
00:04:20,360 --> 00:04:24,280
has taken full control of that area, your presence there becomes much less valuable.

58
00:04:24,280 --> 00:04:27,600
If you still have space behind you to retreat into, use it.

59
00:04:27,600 --> 00:04:31,620
There's no reason to keep testing an area your team no longer has any presence in, especially

60
00:04:31,620 --> 00:04:32,920
when you're on defence.

61
00:04:32,920 --> 00:04:37,180
Your job isn't to take the space back by yourself, it's to stay alive, continue slowing them

62
00:04:37,180 --> 00:04:41,380
down and keep running time off the clock, but you need to be alive to do this.

63
00:04:41,380 --> 00:04:45,360
Where blue team is all about trying to get one big coordinated push to break through,

64
00:04:45,360 --> 00:04:46,840
red is more about survivability.

65
00:04:50,360 --> 00:05:03,360
The previous clip was a good example of what Scout should be doing when his team doesn't have much presence in the area, but when your team is established and actually occupying the space you're defending, your role changes.

66
00:05:03,360 --> 00:05:20,599
Now your job is to prevent threats from reaching your team by pestering them or killing them like this sniper I focused in on to prevent him landing an easy headshot on my heavy or med and clean up easy picks on enemies your power class is already engaging with and safely apply chip damage that forces blue to retreat or delays their push

67
00:05:21,119 --> 00:05:27,079
You're still trying to have as much impact as possible, but without overcommitting and throwing away the position your team has already established.

68
00:05:33,519 --> 00:05:52,199
Another question I got was how you're meant to play scout and narrow maps like Dust Bowl.

69
00:05:52,199 --> 00:05:55,479
Honestly maps like Dust Bowl and Gold Rush are where you need to take all of this advice

70
00:05:55,479 --> 00:05:56,479
the most seriously.

71
00:05:56,479 --> 00:06:01,679
They're old, restrictive maps where the balances age pretty badly, especially for

72
00:06:01,679 --> 00:06:02,679
red.

73
00:06:02,679 --> 00:06:07,639
be anywhere from 18 to 22 seconds and the points can be captured absurdly quickly. And

74
00:06:07,639 --> 00:06:10,919
because of that, there isn't some secret flank or defensive strategy that suddenly

75
00:06:10,919 --> 00:06:15,299
makes Scout amazing. You have to be extremely deliberate about where you position yourself,

76
00:06:15,299 --> 00:06:18,799
which fights you take, and whether you are actually where your team needs you.

77
00:06:18,799 --> 00:06:22,879
High ground is still important, but only if it's actually safe. Most of the obvious

78
00:06:22,879 --> 00:06:27,719
high ground on the first section of Dust Bowl leaves you exposed to snipers and spam. Sometimes

79
00:06:27,719 --> 00:06:31,999
you genuinely have to play around the point and be prepared to back up once your team

80
00:06:31,999 --> 00:06:37,359
loses that space. In this clip I move around and I help my team defend the choke but I always end

81
00:06:37,359 --> 00:06:42,299
up returning to the same flank besides the point. It gives me cover, close range fights and an easy

82
00:06:42,299 --> 00:06:46,719
escape while still allowing me to directly defend the point. It's also a really common place for

83
00:06:46,719 --> 00:06:50,279
blue to put a teleporter so it's naturally somewhere scout can find a lot of value.

84
00:06:50,799 --> 00:06:55,259
Target priority is also important. In general if you see something like a direct hit soldier

85
00:06:55,259 --> 00:06:58,779
there's a good chance he's using it because he wants to destroy your team's sentry nest.

86
00:06:58,779 --> 00:07:04,439
Scout could choose his fights so you should use that freedom to focus the people who are most threatening to your team.

87
00:07:04,539 --> 00:07:08,419
You need to play as a team better than Blue does on old maps like Gold Rush and Dust Bowl.

88
00:07:08,679 --> 00:07:14,339
Something I like doing is briefly moving into Blue's territory and getting someone's attention and then immediately retreating.

89
00:07:14,819 --> 00:07:19,319
Impatient players will often overreact and chase you too far which brings them directly into your team.

90
00:07:19,319 --> 00:07:24,119
That's what happens to this Pyro when he follows me up the stairs just then and my soldier friend already

91
00:07:24,499 --> 00:07:28,079
was holding that position behind me and I knew that so he deals with the Pyro straight away.

92
00:07:28,079 --> 00:07:33,319
I'm not just running away from the fight, I'm dragging the pirate into a fight that's much better for my team.

93
00:07:33,319 --> 00:07:47,399
And give Coldouts a scout because I already mentioned this but he Cold Scout for a reason and you often the first person who can see what Blue is doing So make your team aware of anything they deserve to know about it doesn need to be complicated

94
00:07:47,979 --> 00:07:53,819
You know it's just that their combo is coming through the top left flank or pyro is jetpacking towards you or someone is moving

95
00:07:53,819 --> 00:07:58,759
Into your team space even basic information just gives your team time to react and capitalize

